  <title>5.6.8: Lesson Synthesis</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01340</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.6.8: Lesson Synthesis</md:title>
    <md:uuid>53b8917a-ef8f-46ef-9d69-359fb1f28cf5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Equations are useful not only for representing relationships that change exponentially, but also for answering questions about these situations. In this lesson, students used data from a table to construct equations representing exponential growth and decay. They used these equations to answer questions given the context of the situation.</para>
<para>To help students, review the following points:</para>
<list>
  <item> We looked at some situations where negative values (exponents) representing units of times are meaningful. In those cases, a value of –1 might represent 1 year before a particular time when an estimate or census occurred. </item>
  <item> Choosing the appropriate labels and tick marks on the axes is important for correctly displaying a graph. Make sure you select intervals that can help differentiate among points but still allow for the display of every point in the data. </item>
  <item> When trying to compare values that are in decimal form and scientific notation, first convert all the values into one form or the other. </item>
  <item> Values written in scientific notation can be used to solve problems in context just like values in decimal form. </item>
</list></section>
</content>
